---
// The app's privacy policy. Keep it in step with what the app collects: see
// the event types in the README and the tables in migrations/0001_init.sql.
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import { event } from "@/data/event";

export const prerender = true;

const updated = "November 3, 2026";
const mail = `mailto:${event.contactEmail}`;
---

<Layout title="Privacy Policy" description={`How the ${event.name} conference app uses your information.`}>
  <article class="prose prose-sm mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-8 text-foreground sm:prose-base prose-headings:text-foreground prose-p:text-foreground prose-li:text-foreground prose-strong:text-foreground prose-a:text-primary" data-testid="page-privacy">
    <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
    <p class="text-muted-foreground">Last updated {updated}</p>

    <p>
      This policy covers the {event.name} conference app at <a href={event.appUrl}>app.techseoconnect.com</a> ("the app"), run by {event.name} ("we").
      It explains what the app collects, why, who it's shared with, and the choices you have. The conference website, <a href={event.website}>techseoconnect.com</a>,
      and ticketing through Luma have their own policies.
    </p>

    <h2>What the app collects</h2>

    <h3>If you sign in</h3>
    <p>
      Sign-in is optional and is handled by Clerk, with Google or your email address. We receive your name, email address, profile photo and when you signed up
      and last signed in. You can use the schedule, places and sponsors without signing in.
    </p>

    <h3>Saved talks and places</h3>
    <p>
      Talks and places you save with the heart are kept in your browser. If you're signed in, they're also kept with your account, so they're on every device you
      sign in on.
    </p>

    <h3>Meet</h3>
    <p>
      Meet is optional. If you join, you give a name, a title, up to three topics and, if you like, a link to your LinkedIn profile. Your answers and your profile
      photo are shown to other signed-in attendees who have joined Meet, so people who share your topics can find you and message you on LinkedIn. Your topics can't
      be changed once saved. If you leave Meet, your answers are deleted and no longer shown; we keep only which topics you picked, so they stay the same if you
      join again.
    </p>

    <h3>How the app is used</h3>
    <p>
      The app keeps a log of what happens in it, so the organizers can see which talks and places are popular and how the app is used: the pages you open and the
      things you tap (saving, directions, opening a place or a Meet topic, tapping a LinkedIn link), with the time, a random ID kept in your browser to tell devices
      apart, and your account ID if you're signed in. The same taps, and the pages you open, are also sent to Google Analytics, which uses cookies to count visits.
      See <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" target="_blank" rel="noopener">how Google uses this information</a>.
    </p>

    <h3>Check-in at the door</h3>
    <p>
      To check you in, organizers find you on the conference's Luma guest list (your name, email address and ticket) by name, by email or by scanning the QR code on
      your ticket, and the app records when you were checked in and by whom. To find the right guest, what the organizer typed and the names and email addresses of
      likely matches are sent to an AI service (Anthropic's Claude through OpenRouter, or Cloudflare Workers AI). If you
      register at the door, your name and email are added to the event in Luma, which emails you a ticket.
    </p>

    <h3>AI assistants</h3>
    <p>
      You can connect an AI assistant, such as Claude or ChatGPT, to the app. Once you sign in through the assistant, it can read the schedule, places and sponsors,
      read and change your saved talks and places, and, if you've joined Meet, see your topics and the attendees who share them. We log which of the app's tools the
      assistant used and what it searched for. What you type into the assistant is covered by its own provider's policy.
    </p>

    <h2>Who it's shared with</h2>
    <p>We don't sell your information. It's shared only with the services that run the app, for that purpose:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Cloudflare</strong> hosts the app and its database, and runs Workers AI.</li>
      <li><strong>Clerk</strong> handles sign-in and keeps your account.</li>
      <li><strong>Luma</strong> holds the conference's registrations and tickets.</li>
      <li><strong>Google</strong> provides Google Analytics and the app's fonts.</li>
      <li><strong>OpenRouter</strong> and <strong>Anthropic</strong> match names at the check-in desk.</li>
      <li><strong>MapTiler</strong> provides the map, from OpenStreetMap data.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The conference organizers can see the list of accounts (names, email addresses, when people joined and last signed in, how many talks and places they've saved
      and whether they're on Meet), check-ins, and the app's usage. Directions open in Google Maps or Apple Maps, and LinkedIn links open LinkedIn; those services
      have their own policies.
    </p>

    <h2>How long it's kept</h2>
    <p>
      We keep this information through the conference and may use the usage log, without your account details, to plan future events. You can ask us to delete your
      account at any time: we delete it with your saved talks and places and your Meet answers, and remove your account ID from the usage log.
    </p>

    <h2>Your choices</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Use the app without signing in.</li>
      <li>Leave Meet whenever you like, from the Meet page.</li>
      <li>Block or clear cookies and site data in your browser.</li>
      <li>Email us to see, correct or delete your information.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>The app is for conference attendees and isn't meant for children under 16.</p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>If this policy changes, we'll update it here and change the date at the top.</p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>Questions about this policy or your information: <a href={mail}>{event.contactEmail}</a>.</p>
  </article>
</Layout>
